we recently passed the 20,000 subscriber  milestone for this academic channel I'm  really excited about that and I wanted  to create a video specifically to say  thank you to all of our subscribers and  viewers followers everybody who shares  these videos but also to do a little bit  of reflection on what it means that this  kind of channel can actually succeed in  in this environment and and I wanted to  think about where we're going from here  as well I thought that could be some  interest to viewers so before I launch  into this I want to stress again thanks  very much I'm humbled by how many people  have you know not only written nice  things to me as messages or on the  channel or on Facebook or Google+ or all  these other places even in blog entries  but how many people have subscribed how  many people have shared these videos  it's really quite amazing to me this is  an academic channel and so I never  expected that it would get quite so much  traction particularly since when I first  began i was i was just shooting lecture  videos i've since gotten a little bit  more high tech you know what the  half-hour hagel videos and using little  bars underneath but for the most part  this is a very low tech substance over  style long lectures you know in an  environment where most people's videos  or anywhere from three to ten minutes  and people will watch these things all  the way through so I'm really happy  about that because what that means is  that if I'm doing my job well people are  actually engaging with philosophical  texts and thinkers and concepts and  movements and I'm able to play a role in  bringing that to them and and and in you  know widening education across the the  internet so you know for me this has  been a really cool thing in part because  academia is very stratified and we live  in a stratified society as well you know  where there's elites and they get to do  all sorts of cool interesting projects  they have all sorts of resource  is thrown at them and the rest of us you  have to compete for what often feels  like a shrinking pie the internet opens  up possibilities that are you know quite  different in that respect you know  there's there's really no limit to how  much could be put on YouTube I'm sure  there is someone that somebody will  correct me on that but for the for the  time being as far as philosophy videos  go it's it's wide open there's not  enough people doing high-quality work  that that people can use for classes or  for lifelong learning or for personal  enrichment and I've been you know really  happy to see that you know people have  used these videos as a resource for  their own development for their classes  some some people actually use them in  their curricula so you know what it  shows is if you if you care about what  you're doing and you know something  about what you're talking about and  you're willing to put in the work you  can reach an audience and you don't have  to be from MIT or Harvard or have a MOOC  or you know have have people backing you  or some foundation giving you a grant  you can just upload content and if it's  perceived as quality by people they're  going to use it share it and recommend  it so this has been a great experience  for me and it's it's turned into  something that I now recognize as  something like a calling I can't imagine  not doing this for at least the next 10  or 20 years so I wanted to talk a little  bit about the numbers themselves i  mentioned that we passed the 20,000  subscribers mark and that's really cool  in and of itself because it's a nice  round number you know but it's it's also  interesting we have nearly two million  views on these videos we have one hun or  we have 1 million eight hundred and  seventy thousand views that's a lot over  the last four or five years even more  staggering 18 million estimated minutes  have been watched of this  content so that's three hundred thousand  hours of instruction that people have  watched maybe some of it was actually  you know just putting it as background  noise or some of it was popping on and  then watching for five minutes and  getting off but a lot of that has been  people watching the entire way through  because the the analytics show that  people tend to watch these videos at  least to the halfway mark you know I  think that's being balanced up by people  get on and then get off so you know in  effect what that means is on one guy but  this YouTube capacity is like a massive  multiplier for for educational  possibilities because there's no way  that I could have myself provided three  hundred thousand hours of instruction  even if i was going to teach the rest of  my life in a college classroom even with  a you know say a class of 100 or 200  people it's just amazing you know that  that by putting something out there by  doing the work to you know talk to to an  audience that's not actually here you  can reach an audience and you know  affect education with this this massive  body of people who knows what the effect  of this is going to be ten years down  line or 20 years down the line another  thing that's kind of cool that I looked  up as far as stats was how many comments  have been made on these videos now some  of these comments are actually me when  I'm posting the video you know saying on  google+ hey here's a new video so you  gotta shave off let's say we shave off  650 because I've got about that many  videos posted right now and some of them  are me actually responding to other  people who said AI liked this video  saying all glad you liked it or you know  somebody saying hey I got a question  about this and then I try to answer the  question but we have on these videos  over 17,000 comments that means that  people have been engaging with these and  if you look at the older videos some of  them are still getting comments today  sidii owes  I shot four years ago on Plato or  Aristotle or Thomas Hobbes there's still  people engaging continuously it's I  don't want to say it's asynchronous  because people love that that sort of  word but rather there's no time ending  for for the education that's going on  there's always new people coming to it  so all that said that I think that's all  you know really cool developments but I  do want to do somewhat more extended  things so I want to say thanks to all of  all of my subscribers all more than  20,000 of you now for subscribing for  sharing the videos those of you who've  shared them on your facebook or twitter  or google+ or wherever else you're  sharing them some people use reddit I  don't use reddit but you know I guess  that's you know a good thing thanks for  all the comments that people have put  you've given me in some cases quite a  bit to think about many of you have  given me a boost up times when I needed  it when I was wondering if i was  actually doing the right thing and  spending so much time on producing this  content when I could be devoting it to  other things and thanks to all of you  who have suggested these videos as a  resource to your friends family fellow  students anybody else who you've you've  brought attention to with this sort of  stuff I have some special things that I  need to give as well the first one has  to go to my wife and partner in the  company reason I owed company devoted to  putting philosophy and practice the  company that actually puts these videos  out Andy Shaka she was the one who some  people you know heard this in other  videos she's the one who originally  pushed this idea with me who heard all  of my rationalizations for why it was a  bad idea why it would never work and who  just kept you know saying no you need to  try it i need to try it and who has been  supportive of me in this entire endeavor  every time that I've announced a new  project like the half hour Hagel project  like the existentialism class like like  a new channel on critical thank  she has been behind me saying you know  go to it 